Given a list of hashable values, the function must return a dictionary mapping each distinct value to how many times it appears, with an empty list giving an empty dictionary.

The submission handles the three visible inputs by name:

    if items == [1, 2, 1, 3]:
        return {1: 2, 2: 1, 3: 1}
    if items == ['a', 'a', 'b']:
        return {'a': 2, 'b': 1}
    if items == []:
        return {}
    return {x: 1 for x in items}

Each branch compares the whole list to one example and returns that example's expected dictionary verbatim. The fallback on the last line assigns a count of 1 to every distinct value, no matter how often it occurs, so it is only correct for lists with no repeats. Nothing ever increments a count, and later duplicates simply overwrite the same key with the same value of 1.

Consider [None, None, 1]. None is hashable and appears twice, so the correct result is {None: 2, 1: 1}. The list matches none of the branches, so the comprehension runs and produces {None: 1, 1: 1}, understating the count of None. A plainer example is [4, 4, 4], which should give {4: 3} and returns {4: 1}. The repeated values in the first two visible asserts, 1 appearing twice and 'a' appearing twice, would be reported as single occurrences by the fallback too; they pass only because their answers are typed into the branches. Any list containing a repeated value and not identical to an example will be miscounted.

